            <ArticleTitle>Lesson of the month 1: Obesity hypoventilation (Pickwickian) syndrome: a reversible cause of severe pulmonary hypertension.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>578-581</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.7861/clinmedicine.17-6-578</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Obesity hypoventilation syndrome (OHS) is a condition in which an individual with a body mass index &gt;30 kg/m2 develops daytime alveolar hypoventilation (defined as a resting PaCO2 &gt;45 mmHg) that cannot be attributed to other pathologies. It is a condition with increasing prevalence and rising cost to healthcare systems worldwide. Right heart failure and pulmonary hypertension are well-known complications of this syndrome. Here, we present the case of a female patient with OHS who presented to our centre with severe pulmonary hypertension, which resolved with appropriate treatment. We also review this clinical condition and its diagnosis and management.</AbstractText>
